# Rotation for the proxy accounting logs that server.py tails for traffic
# billing (see the "Traffic accounting framework" section in the root README).
#
# Runs as the unprivileged service user (user1) from that user's crontab, so it
# carries its own state file and needs no root — see ops/README below.
#
# copytruncate is mandatory: the writers live inside Docker containers
#   - ssserver, via the container's `... >> <log>` shell redirect (O_APPEND)
#   - private_socks.py, via open(path, "a")            (O_APPEND)
# and hold the file open for the container's lifetime. A rename-style rotation
# would leave them writing to the old inode (new file stays empty). copytruncate
# truncates in place; because both writers use O_APPEND, their next write lands
# at the real end (offset 0), never creating a sparse hole. server.py's tailer
# notices size < its saved offset and resumes reading from 0.
#
# The log *content* is disposable — server.py has already folded each line into
# traffic.sqlite3 in real time — so retention is short and purely forensic.

/home/user1/serverfetch/Plugins/shadowsocks-ios-mode/state/logs/*.log
/home/user1/serverfetch/Plugins/private-mac-vpn/state/logs/*.log {
    daily
    maxsize 50M
    rotate 3
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}
